in our lives and in the world around us. And actually to get started, just with kind of a

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:09.240
quick illustration of that. I wonder how many of you here, if you want to, you can raise your hand,

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.920
you don't have to, but how many of you are familiar with this historical period called

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:19.440
the Troubles in Northern Ireland? Okay, there's like a couple of you that know what that is. Okay,

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.980
it's all right. Some of you, when I describe it, you'll maybe be like, oh, okay, yeah, I've heard

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:28.120
of that before. There's this period in Northern Ireland known as the Troubles, and this started

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:35.160
around the 1960s, and it started with this conflict about what the future of Northern

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:40.820
Ireland would be. And it's something that really kind of took over that whole country for about

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:46.840
30 years, where there was this ongoing conflict. And it was a conflict between two parties,

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:52.680
two groups of people. One of those were the nationalists or the Republicans, who were mostly

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:57.580
Catholic. And these were people who wanted a united Ireland, who wanted Ireland and Northern

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:03.320
Ireland to be in unity together. And the other party were the unionists or the loyalists. These

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:08.440
were mostly Protestant, and these are people who wanted Northern Ireland to remain part of the

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:12.800
United Kingdom. And so there was this whole conflict over this, and it wasn't just like a

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:18.200
political conflict, like politicians shaking their fingers at each other. It erupted into urban

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:24.740
warfare, where paramilitary groups like the Irish Republican Army or the Ulster Volunteer Force,

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:30.460
You know, there were just consistent attacks, car bombings, shootings, all these kinds of

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:34.340
things happening over that 30-year period with different spikes and then like kind of

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:35.640
a lull and then another spike.

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:38.820
But it was a consistent period of violence for three decades.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:45.260
Over 3,600 people were killed during the Troubles and estimates of between 30,000 and 50,000

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:47.460
additional injuries during that time.

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:48.840
So this was not a small thing.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:52.280
This was something that really took over this whole nation.

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:59.500
And in that time, neighborhoods became segregated, and they were segregated along those party

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:03.480
lines, where if, you know, people that were part of these different parties wouldn't live

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:08.800
in the same neighborhood with each other, they kind of blocked off these neighborhoods

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:10.540
with what they called peace lines.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:14.280
But actually, what a peace line meant is if you cross it, there's not going to be any

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:14.640
peace.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:15.900
Like, you're going to get attacked.

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:19.080
You might get killed if you go into the wrong neighborhood.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:26.180
So it was an incredibly divisive time in Northern Ireland's history, and political solutions didn't seem to be going anywhere.

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:29.920
Over these decades, politicians tried to solve these problems.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:35.180
They tried to start negotiations, but anything that they tried just stalled out, collapsed,

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:37.540
because everyone's just so suspicious of each other.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.560
Nobody trusts each other to be true to their word, and so no solutions really went anywhere.

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:48.960
And during these times, I mentioned that one of these parties was predominantly Protestant and the other predominantly Catholic,

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:57.340
It wasn't necessarily a religious conflict, but it was more of a political or a social

00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:00.000
conflict, and yet they kind of broke down into those groups.

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:05.460
And so even a lot of the institutional churches, Protestant and Catholic churches, would kind

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:11.380
of buy into that tribalism of their surrounding society and sort of pick sides and kind of

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:14.980
see the others as enemies, and all that kind of stuff was going on as well.

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:21.060
But at the same time, there were believers in Christ in that country who wanted to live differently,

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:22.800
who wanted to see a different solution.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:28.340
So, for instance, there was one group called the Cori Mila community that actually became a space

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:32.340
where Protestants and Catholics from warring neighborhoods would get together.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:36.720
They would live, they would eat, they would pray, they would dialogue together

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:40.100
and really find unity through faith in Christ.

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:44.740
And this was a community that started to bring some healing to the nation.

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:50.020
Along with that, many clergy people at that time, on both sides, Catholic and Protestant,

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:57.640
would kind of act behind the scenes to open lines of communication and negotiation between these warring factions.

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:03.120
And so they would talk with opposing politicians, they would talk with these opposing paramilitary groups

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:08.660
and try to kind of bring people together as trusted intermediaries in those conflicts.

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:11.920
So the clergy kind of stepped up to try to play those roles.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:21.980
And most powerfully, most substantially, there were many followers of Jesus at that time who chose to publicly forgive those who had murdered their family members.

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:24.060
Came out and publicly said that.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:33.540
And in doing so, became a powerful witness of the gospel to bring reconciliation and restoration of those relationships and in those communities.

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:41.780
And the fruit of these believers living out their faith and demonstrating the gospel in this way led to actually real change in this country.

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:48.160
That culminated specifically in what was called the Good Friday Agreement that was made on April 10th in 1998.

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:51.620
This was kind of a huge turning point in this conflict,

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:56.700
that after 30 years of violence and bitterness and division and hatred,

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:59.520
that finally these parties came together and said,

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:02.820
we're going to make peace and we're going to put an end to this violence.

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:05.620
And there were still some things that happened after that,

00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:10.500
but that largely brought about the end of this period known as the Troubles.

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:15.280
Now, I share that today partly because I think it's just a great testimony of the power of

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:19.680
the gospel to bring real change in the lives of people and even in communities.

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:23.400
But at the same time, as we think about that, and I want us to know there is that power,

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:28.820
we also recognize that in a fallen world, these kind of political agreements come and

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:29.000
go.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:34.820
It doesn't mean that there's no more issues, that it will never flare up again, or that

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:36.240
everything is totally solved.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:41.420
But today, as we look into our passage in Ephesians 1, we're going to see actually a

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:47.580
greater and more ultimate hope and unity empowered by the gospel message. And we're going to explore

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:52.840
the power of the gospel to deal with the problem of sin in our lives and ultimately unite all

