00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:55.700
knowing what it is that we're saying when we use these terms. And so we want to, all of us, get on

00:13:55.700 --> 00:14:00.340
the same page with that here today. And so let's think about redemption and forgiveness to start.

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:04.880
I'll give you just Greek background of these words real quick. The word translated as redemption,

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:11.700
that's apolitrosis. It means to release or set free with the implied analogy to the process of

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:17.480
freeing a slave. So to set free, to liberate, to deliver, to redeem. So this is specifically

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:23.420
talking about buying someone out of slavery. That's what redemption means. That's what it

00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:28.360
means to redeem. We're not talking about a general idea. We're not talking about maybe like a football

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:32.980
team trying to redeem themselves or something. We're talking about redeeming out of slavery.

00:14:33.220 --> 00:14:38.100
That's what this means. Then if we look at forgiveness, what's that? Where's that come from?

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:44.740
It's a Greek word, aphesis. It means to remove the guilt resulting from wrongdoing. So if somebody

00:14:44.740 --> 00:14:50.320
is guilty because of something they've done wrong, aphesis, forgiveness, that means to remove

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:56.000
that guilt somehow. So when we look into this passage and we're trying to understand what it

00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:02.800
is talking about, it's talking about that this is what Jesus has done for us. This is what happens

